; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; math notation — universal symbolic language
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; Math notation is language-independent: + - * ^ = are the same
; in every human language. These are NOT English bhasha nodes.
;
; Only `=` needs a bhasha node (samikarana signal — equation marker).
; The other operators (+, -, *, ^) are detected structurally by
; the samikarana-bandha tantra after BQG tokenization.
;
; Variables (x, t, etc.) are NOT declared — they are detected as
; any unresolved letter in an equation context (has `=`).
; This keeps the system general: any letter can be a variable.

; ── equation marker ──────────────────────────────────────────────
; `=` signals samikarana (equation). BQG emits [=, samikarana, =].
; This is the ONLY math symbol that needs a bhasha node — it tells
; the pipeline "this is an equation, activate samikarana-bandha."

(bhasha samikarana-chihnah
  (swarupa samikarana)
  (naama =))
